<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Comminges</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1454 CE (approx.) · capital Muret</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pyrenean county around Saint-Bertrand-de-Comminges whose counts fought on the Occitan side in the Albigensian Crusade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Counts of Comminges appear in the early 10th century, governing the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> valley around the old Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugdunum-convenarum">Lugdunum Convenarum</a>, later Saint-Bertrand-de-Comminges. They were related to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/couserans">Couserans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> and drifted into the orbit of the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">Toulouse</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Comminges was a mountainous land of pastoral farming, small towns and pilgrimage, centred on the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugdunum-convenarum">Saint-Bertrand</a>. The counts later held Muret near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">Toulouse</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-iv-of-comminges">Bernard IV</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vi-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VI of Toulouse</a> against the crusaders and was present at the battle of Muret in 1213, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a> was killed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The comital line ended with Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-countess-of-comminges">Marguerite</a>, whose disputed inheritance was settled in favour of the crown; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vii-of-france">Charles VII</a> annexed Comminges to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a> in 1454. The name survives as a region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-garonne">Haute-Garonne</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-comminges.jpg" alt="County of Comminges" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_comt%C3%A9_fr_Comminges_(ancien).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1454 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Muret</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernard IV of Comminges, Marguerite of Comminges</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Comminges</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1177&amp;focus=county-of-comminges" aria-label="County of Comminges on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4712 636 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Comminges lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5032 892L5035 888M5032 892L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5035 888h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5032 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5032 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Muret. <a href="/atlas?year=1177&amp;focus=county-of-comminges">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
